LOGBOOK.md:530:- FINDING: The reverse dependency stays forbidden and is what still kills the memory-only candidate B. Apple, same page: *"you should not rely on this communication channel with the containing app for basic Tunnel Provider operation (start, stop, etc.)."* App→provider may launch; provider→app may never be assumed. The transport selection (candidate D) therefore did not change — only the lifecycle around it.
LOGBOOK.md:537:- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
LOGBOOK.md:596:- ROOT CAUSE: `TunnelProviderAdapter.providerDidFail` was `nonisolated` and deferred *all* admission into a freshly spawned unstructured `Task`. Swift orders nothing between unstructured tasks contending for the same actor, so two sequential provider-failure callbacks could enter `handleProviderFailure` in reverse order. The `cancelTunnelIssuedGeneration` ledger enforced exactly-once but had no notion of *which* call arrived first, so the second error (`internalInvariant` 1009) beat the first (`runtimeStartupFailed` 1007) in **10 of 40** focused runs (~25%).
LOGBOOK.md:597:- FINDING: A generation ledger consulted *inside* the actor cannot recover call order that was already lost at the task-spawn boundary. Ordering has to be captured where the caller still exists — on the caller's thread — not where the work is executed.
LOGBOOK.md:598:- FIX: Added file-private `ProviderFailureAdmission` (`NSLock`-guarded claim flag) in `Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/ProviderLifecycle.swift:461`. `providerDidFail` now claims admission **synchronously** before spawning the task (`ProviderLifecycle.swift:907`); later and concurrent callers are dropped before reaching the actor. `startGeneration` releases the claim when it bumps `latestGeneration` (`ProviderLifecycle.swift:632`), keeping admission generation-scoped exactly as the old ledger was.
LOGBOOK.md:608:- FINDING: The board diff contained **75** removed dependency edges (37 task-to-task, 38 container), not the 17 round 2 reported. The 38 container removals were `task-board repair-links` clearing unsupported container links that also carried four container-level cycles; 34 of them are still enforced at task level, and the 4 that are not are intentional A0/ADR-011 deferrals. Full per-edge ledger with retained-counterpart proofs is attached to the task.
LOGBOOK.md:628:- FINDING: The root cause of the M1 deadlock was a contract/integration inversion, not a milestone-ordering mistake. `TASK-260715-1pn983` (binding memory ledger) was blocked by `30ugfm` (M1 routing integration) and `z37ay7` (M2 UDP enforcement) — both are work the ledger is supposed to *govern*. Removing those two edges and adding `z37ay7 blockedBy 1pn983` broke two cycles and kept the reviewer-added `3miqh4 ← 1pn983` accountability gate intact; `productionAuthorization` stays `false`.
